  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Magic Initiate doesn’t support Artificer as a choice because the Artificer isn’t in the Player’s Handbook. Instead, we have Artificer Initiate. In many ways it’s just Magic Initiate for the Artificer, but you give up one cantrip for a tool proficiency and the ability to use that tool as a spellcasting focus for Intelligence-based spellcasting."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Unfortunately, the only other Intelligence-based spellcasters are the Arcane Trickster Rogue, the Eldritch Knight Fighter, and the Wizard. Even for those classes, the benefits of a tool proficiency and using a tool as a spellcasting focus are very minor. You might get a magical tool that provides a bonus to your spell attacks and save DCs, but there are plenty of focuses which do that already and don’t require a feat."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The real benefit of Artificer Initiate is buried in the Artificer’s spell list. You get many cantrips available to the sorcerer or the wizard, including staple options like Booming Blade, but the Artificer has access to spells which the wizard doesn’t like Cure Wounds, Faerie Fire, and Sanctuary. Essentially, Artificer Initiate lets you get Intelligence-based spellcasting with access to spells which historically were only available to the bard, the cleric, and the druid. Unfortunately, the the classes which benefit are all Intelligence-based spellcasters who use the Wizard’s spell list."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "For more information about spellcasting feats, including ratings of the various feats, see our "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Spellcasting Feats Guide",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/feats/spellcasting-feats/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
